WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.040
A whole new generation of moviegoers&nbsp;
is getting introduced to Godzilla.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.600
Everyone knows a little something about the&nbsp;
legendary lizard, but fewer are familiar with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.040
titanic catalogue of films. If you're wondering&nbsp;
where you should — or shouldn't — get started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.560
here are the best and worst&nbsp;
movies in the history of Godzilla.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.360
There's a reason the most recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.480
Godzilla-headlining film, King of the&nbsp;
Monsters, features the foursome of Godzilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.320
Mothra, Rodan, and King Ghidorah, and much of&nbsp;
it can be traced back to this colossal classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.200
released ten years after the first Godzilla film.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.480
In his first appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.680
King Ghidorah hatches on Earth to rain&nbsp;
down destruction with its three heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.640
two tails, and lightning breath. Mankind's only&nbsp;
hope is a team-up of Godzilla, Rodan, and Mothra.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.200
It's a lot of fun, and the kaiju carnage is&nbsp;
some of the most powerful in the franchise.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.080
Godzilla was a massive success&nbsp;
upon its release in 1954,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.880
so of course it made sense for Toho to push out&nbsp;
a sequel to the film as quickly as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.960
That arrived the following year in&nbsp;
the form of Godzilla Raids Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.760
and as is the case with a lot of quickly&nbsp;
made sequels, the results are disappointing.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:18.000
For one thing, though this is the first film to&nbsp;
feature monster vs. monster combat as Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.680
battles fellow dinosaur-like creature Anguirus,&nbsp;
the battles are not yet what they would later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.400
become. There's a lot of shuffling around,&nbsp;
much of it an effort to look animalistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.280
but it's just not as fun as the full-on wrestling&nbsp;
matches that would follow in future films.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.840
The mythology is also a little underwhelming,&nbsp;
particularly when you find out that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.280
Godzilla is actually just another giant&nbsp;
radioactive lizard from the same species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.840
since they killed the original monster at the&nbsp;
end of the first film. The film also suffers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.960
technically, featuring a good deal less atmosphere&nbsp;
and visual artistry than its predecessor.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.160
All that said, Godzilla Raids&nbsp;
Again is still watchable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.480
but it's the definition of a lackluster sequel.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.440
The Heisei era of Godzilla films kicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.560
off with an attempt to return the character&nbsp;
to its atomic horror roots with the somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.800
divisive The Return of Godzilla in 1984, and&nbsp;
things remained rather dark with Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.560
vs. Biollante in 1989. Those are both&nbsp;
solid films, but the Heisei period really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.240
picked up steam with its third entry, which&nbsp;
managed to maintain a relatively serious tone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.880
while also developing its own version of the Showa&nbsp;
era's "throw everything at the screen" approach.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.960
The story begins with the notion that Godzilla&nbsp;
actually first appeared to humans as a smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.560
less mutated dinosaur version of himself in 1944,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.680
when he saved a group of World War II soldiers.&nbsp;
A group of time travelers from the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.720
embark on a mission to stop the dinosaur from&nbsp;
being irradiated and thus turning into Godzilla.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.120
"Got him … take that you dinosaur."

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.360
It turns out, though, that their real&nbsp;
mission was to swap the dinosaur out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.000
three smaller creatures who would then&nbsp;
be irradiated to create… King Ghidorah.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.520
Things only get more convoluted from there, but&nbsp;
what all of this essentially builds to is a battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.840
between Godzilla and Mecha King Ghidorah, a&nbsp;
version of the three-headed monster that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.960
been mechanically modified by people from the&nbsp;
future to give it one cybernetic head and a host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.840
of other gadgets. The whole film is a blast,&nbsp;
but it's worth it for the final battle alone.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.760
For a little while in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.360
middle of the Showa era of Godzilla films, Toho&nbsp;
experimented with broadening the franchise's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.160
appeal through the introduction of various new&nbsp;
characters. At the same time, they were trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.560
to keep budgets low, which unfortunately&nbsp;
brings us to Godzilla's son, Minilla.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.320
There's nothing inherently wrong&nbsp;
with Godzilla having offspring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.200
In fact, later films also toyed with this&nbsp;
idea to varying degrees of success. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.360
though, the interactions between Godzilla&nbsp;
and Minilla are played with such a comedic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.840
put-upon-father sensibility that it&nbsp;
starts to grate on you almost immediately.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.520
That's not the only problem with the film,&nbsp;
though. The creatures this time around — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.960
giant mantises and the giant spider called&nbsp;
Kumonga - look cool, but because they're puppets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.840
instead of men in suits, the fight scenes&nbsp;
are rather limited in terms of intensity.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.960
"Godzilla, Mothra and King Ghidorah:&nbsp;
Giant Monsters All-Out Attack."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.360
What a title! It's one of the best introductions&nbsp;
you could hope for to a Godzilla film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.760
because it tells you exactly what you're about to&nbsp;
see without spoiling how you're going to see it.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.480
Like many other entries in Godzilla's&nbsp;
Millennium era, Giant Monsters All-Out Attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.800
functions as a direct sequel to the original&nbsp;
1954 film, ignoring all previous installments.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.760
That means this film once again frames&nbsp;
Godzilla as a villain hellbent on destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.400
and once again depicts the first meeting between&nbsp;
Godzilla and two of his greatest co-stars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.160
Mothra and King Ghidorah.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.600
In this film, the latter two monsters, as well&nbsp;
as Baragon in a smaller role, are framed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.640
"guardian" monsters who have to be awakened to&nbsp;
save the world from Godzilla, and the twists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.840
turns that result from this mythos help drive&nbsp;
the film even beyond its fun kaiju battles.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.880
"The guardian monsters are&nbsp;
here… they're all here."

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.440
The visual effects are a little dated&nbsp;
today, but the clash of these titans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.560
still remains a sight to behold. This movie is&nbsp;
pure monster fun, and proof that sometimes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.200
need to wipe the slate clean to build something&nbsp;
new within a seemingly immortal franchise.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.080
All Monsters Attack, also known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.920
as Godzilla's Revenge, is the story of a young&nbsp;
boy named Ichiro who dreams of Monster Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.440
to escape his lonely, bullied life. In his&nbsp;
dreams he befriends Godzilla's son Minilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.000
and the two deal with their respective&nbsp;
bullies through the power of friendship.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:49.120
"Hey, come on over here. I won't hurt ya."

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.880
This isn't necessarily a bad concept, but the film&nbsp;
also leans heavily on footage of Godzilla that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:01.040
been recycled from Son of Godzilla and Ebirah:&nbsp;
Horror of the Deep, among others. There's also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.080
problem of Minilla, who remains annoying despite&nbsp;
his noble quest to overcome his bully. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.240
nothing necessarily wrong with the premise,&nbsp;
but the execution leaves a lot to be desired.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.680
The final film in the Heisei period of Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.480
films had to go out with a bang, and while that&nbsp;
could have been just another monster team-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.920
movie, what we got is one of the most moving&nbsp;
and ambitious films in the entire franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.520
The film hinges on the discovery that the original&nbsp;
"Oxygen Destroyer" device used to kill Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.200
in the very first film also mutated a group of&nbsp;
prehistoric sea creatures into giant crustacean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.800
monsters, which then merge into one super-monster&nbsp;
that basically looks like the devil itself.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.280
Godzilla vs. Destoroyah has it&nbsp;
all: Godzilla in ultimate peril,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:51.120
and a monster antagonist that's both legitimately&nbsp;
creepy and appropriately massive. It even somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:57.120
manages to pull off a "Godzilla Junior" plot&nbsp;
that really lands. It all culminates in a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.400
effective finale that shows us the death and&nbsp;
rebirth of Godzilla in a way no other film has.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.760
In the 1970s, Toho was hoping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.560
create its own giant hero modeled after&nbsp;
the success of the Japanese character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.280
Ultraman, and held a contest asking&nbsp;
children to design their own hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.880
The resulting character, after&nbsp;
plenty of tweaks by the studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.400
was the giant robot Jet Jaguar, who was originally&nbsp;
supposed to get his own film. Somewhere along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:30.800
way the Jet Jaguar film was merged with a Godzilla&nbsp;
film, and Godzilla vs. Megalon is the result.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.480
Godzilla and a giant robot man in the same movie&nbsp;
definitely sounds like fun, but the film itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.880
suffers from a rushed production process,&nbsp;
some of the less impressive monster designs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.600
in the franchise, and a plot that spends more&nbsp;
time with Jet Jaguar than you end up wanting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.800
The film is also infamous for its depiction&nbsp;
of Godzilla executing the most fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.840
ridiculous drop kick in movie history, and the&nbsp;
monster fights don't really improve beyond that.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.440
Yes, the very first Godzilla movie is still among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:04.560
the best, and not just because it's where our&nbsp;
favorite giant radioactive lizard originated. Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.160
when his story began, Godzilla was a metaphor for&nbsp;
post-atomic Japanese anxieties about the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.480
Director Ishiro Honda leans into that metaphor&nbsp;
with an atmospheric, dread-filled story about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:22.000
nation haunted by the specter of something with&nbsp;
massive implications and destructive potential.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.080
Honda took particular time to focus&nbsp;
in on the consequences of this giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:31.520
monster's destruction, and it remains an absolute&nbsp;
classic more than six decades after its release.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.920
Yes, director Roland Emmerich's 1998 Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.960
film, the first American attempt at a reboot for&nbsp;
the creature, gets a lot of hate thrown its way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.000
People don't like the creature design, they don't&nbsp;
like Matthew Broderick cast as the scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.400
poised to take down Godzilla, and sometimes&nbsp;
they just don't like the 1990s of it all.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.160
"Ugh, tell me that's not another parade."

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.240
Despite all of those reasons, though,&nbsp;
the biggest problem with Emmerich's film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.040
is the frustration that comes from watching it,&nbsp;
because if you're really paying attention you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.000
the sense that there was almost, maybe,&nbsp;
possibly a hint of a good movie lurking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.560
in there somewhere before everything went wrong.&nbsp;
There are some interesting moments in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.120
but they aren't enough to carry the film&nbsp;
even as a campy "so bad it's good" movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.360
It's just a series of confrontations with a&nbsp;
monster so things can explode for a few minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.520
before the film sets things up for the&nbsp;
next set of explosions, and then it ends.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:32.800
After the success of Legendary's Godzilla&nbsp;
in 2014, Toho decided to reboot its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.000
Godzilla franchise with possibly the&nbsp;
scariest incarnation of Godzilla ever.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:44.480
Co-directed by Neon Genesis Evangelion's Hideaki&nbsp;
Anno and Attack on Titan's Shinji Higuchi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.080
Shin Godzilla's kaiju is entirely new,&nbsp;
as if the 1954 version never existed.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.320
The movie features sharp satire of the&nbsp;
Japanese government as it deals with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:10:00.000
creature arising from the sea and evolving into&nbsp;
an enormous, needle-toothed monstrosity with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.640
beady eyes and a devastating pulse attack&nbsp;
that comes out of its mouth and spines.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:10.000
"This doesn't qualify as an armed attack.&nbsp;
And to be accurate, article 76 stipulates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.360
'by an aggressor country or an&nbsp;
equivalent force' — not some monster."

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.000
"We don't have time to argue over semantics."

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.120
Godzilla here returns to his roots as a&nbsp;
metaphor for mass disaster — in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:26.800
the 2011 earthquake, tsunami, and&nbsp;
subsequent nuclear meltdown at Fukushima.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.080
The population's reactions&nbsp;
to Godzilla are realistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:35.120
as are the scenes of destruction caused as&nbsp;
he morphs from one hideous form to the next.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:40.720
The way Godzilla is depicted makes it appear&nbsp;
that his existence is one of excruciating pain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:44.320
While the government dithers in&nbsp;
endless meetings and scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.800
struggle to come up with a way to stop&nbsp;
Godzilla, he slowly lumbers forward as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.640
a walking metaphor for disaster that&nbsp;
could always strike at any moment.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:57.040
Godzilla: The Planet Eater is&nbsp;
considered a disappointing end to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:00.640
an anime trilogy that began with 2017's Godzilla:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:06.080
Planet of the Monsters and continued with&nbsp;
2018's Godzilla: City on the Edge of Battle.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.200
The main protagonist has a&nbsp;
big grudge against Godzilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:14.400
and with good reason, as Godzilla and other&nbsp;
kaiju have emerged on Earth and taken it over.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:20.800
Earth has also been visited by not one, but two&nbsp;
alien species who can't manage to defeat Godzilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.280
foiling their plans to invade the Earth.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.480
Having all fled the rampaging Godzilla on&nbsp;
a spaceship, the humans and aliens return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.760
two decades later to find that not only&nbsp;
have 20,000 years passed on Earth, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:39.840
that both Earth and Godzilla have significantly&nbsp;
changed, with Godzilla DNA present everywhere.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.320
The philosophical underpinnings of the&nbsp;
trilogy's end are somewhat downbeat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.480
Humanity can best survive if it&nbsp;
reverts to a more primitive lifestyle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.640
eschewing technology and submitting&nbsp;
to the will of gods and monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:58.960
both literal and not. Suffice to say, it all makes&nbsp;
for an unorthodox take on the Godzilla story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:03.600
one that appears not to have resonated with&nbsp;
as many viewers as the monster's best movies.

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.960
This chapter of Legendary's MonsterVerse&nbsp;
not only ruled the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:12.960
but it also has the second-highest&nbsp;
audience score for a Godzilla movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.880
behind Godzilla vs. Destoroyah,&nbsp;
according to Rotten Tomatoes.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.800
Godzilla vs. Kong promises an epic battle between&nbsp;
the titular kaiju, and delivers with several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:27.680
rounds of ferocious fighting as Godzilla and&nbsp;
Kong vie for the right to be the one true king.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.800
To borrow from Saturday Night Live's Stefon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:35.520
this movie has everything: giant&nbsp;
monsters brawling, a creepy mech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:41.680
awesome tech, beautiful special effects, Easter&nbsp;
eggs galore, and... mostly irrelevant humans.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:46.320
The film is tons of fun, and in the tradition&nbsp;
of many Godzilla films, what the human beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.960
are doing generally takes a back seat to&nbsp;
the spectacle of two of popular culture's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:55.360
best-known beasts whaling on each other.&nbsp;
It's no wonder that fans of the genre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.840
and fans of blockbuster action in general&nbsp;
have responded so positively to it.

00:13:01.241 --> 00:13:06.080
Ebirah, Horror of the Deep, also known&nbsp;
as Godzilla vs. the Sea Monster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:12.880
Godzilla – Ebirah – Mothra: Big Duel in the South&nbsp;
Seas, begins the stage of Godzilla's Showa era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:15.760
when Godzilla gets silly and Toho cuts costs.

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:21.120
The film features a friendlier-looking Godzilla&nbsp;
fighting what's basically a giant lobster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:26.240
Originally conceived as a vehicle for King&nbsp;
Kong, the project was retooled when Toho&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:32.000
and Rankin/Bass, which produced a King Kong&nbsp;
cartoon, couldn't come to terms on Kong's use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:36.720
Well, not really "retooled" so much as just&nbsp;
sticking Godzilla in where Kong would appear.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:41.440
The movie is a jumble of spy shenanigans,&nbsp;
terrorists called the Red Bamboo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:47.040
enslaving islanders, a damsel in distress, and&nbsp;
a criminal who reforms after his misadventures.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:51.120
Godzilla doesn't just fight&nbsp;
Ebirah, but Mothra, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:56.480
as she arrives to save her people, all before the&nbsp;
island is nuked out of existence. The movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:02.320
slow-paced and it takes a long time for Godzilla&nbsp;
to appear, even by Godzilla film standards.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:07.600
Where other Godzilla movies evoke bewilderment&nbsp;
and terror at the awesome power of nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:12.960
this movie has a scene where Godzilla and Ebirah&nbsp;
play ping-pong with a rock. It's... not good.

